# Centralised Logging with OpenTelemetry and Loki

## Overview

The payment platform uses the **OpenTelemetry Collector** for centralised Kubernetes log collection and **Grafana Loki** as the log storage backend.

The final implemented logging architecture was:

```text
Spring Boot / Kubernetes Pods
          |
          | stdout / stderr
          v
Container Runtime
          |
          v
EKS Worker Node
/var/log/pods/...
          |
          | hostPath mount
          v
OpenTelemetry Collector
      DaemonSet
          |
          | filelog receiver
          v
     Logs Pipeline
          |
          +--> processors
          |
          v
   OTLP/HTTP Exporter
          |
          v
        Loki
          |
          v
       Grafana
```

This logging pipeline was successfully verified end-to-end.

---

# Why OpenTelemetry Collector?

The objective was to use OpenTelemetry as the central telemetry collection layer where practical.

The Collector provides a common architecture based on:

```text
Receiver
   |
   v
Processor
   |
   v
Exporter
```

Each component has a specific responsibility.

### Receiver

A receiver determines **how telemetry enters the Collector**.

For Kubernetes container logs, the `filelog` receiver was used.

### Processor

Processors operate on telemetry after it has been received.

They can perform operations such as:

- memory protection
- batching
- filtering
- attribute manipulation
- Kubernetes metadata enrichment

### Exporter

An exporter determines **where telemetry leaves the Collector**.

For logs, the Collector exports telemetry to Loki using OTLP over HTTP.

---

# Why Run the Collector as a DaemonSet?

Initially the OpenTelemetry Collector was running as a Deployment.

For node-level Kubernetes log collection, it was changed to a **DaemonSet**.

A DaemonSet ensures that Kubernetes attempts to run one Collector pod on each eligible worker node.

```text
EKS Cluster

Worker Node 1
├── Application Pods
└── OTel Collector

Worker Node 2
├── Application Pods
└── OTel Collector

Worker Node 3
├── Application Pods
└── OTel Collector
```

This architecture is useful because Kubernetes container logs exist on the filesystem of the node where the container is running.

Each Collector therefore reads logs from its local worker node.

If the cluster adds another eligible worker node, the DaemonSet can automatically schedule another Collector instance there.

---

# Where Kubernetes Container Logs Come From

Applications running in containers normally write logs to:

```text
stdout
stderr
```

The container runtime captures those streams and Kubernetes makes container log files available on the worker node.

For this implementation, logs were collected from:

```text
/var/log/pods
```

The Collector does not automatically have access to the host filesystem.

Therefore, the node directory must be mounted into the Collector pod.

---

# Mounting Node Logs into the Collector

The OpenTelemetry Helm values configured a `hostPath` volume:

```yaml
mode: daemonset

extraVolumes:
  - name: varlogpods
    hostPath:
      path: /var/log/pods

extraVolumeMounts:
  - name: varlogpods
    mountPath: /var/log/pods
    readOnly: true
```

The flow becomes:

```text
Worker Node

/var/log/pods
      |
      | hostPath
      v
OTel Collector Pod

/var/log/pods
```

The mount is read-only because the Collector only needs to **read** application logs.

It should not modify Kubernetes container log files.

---

# Filelog Receiver

The `filelog` receiver reads the container log files mounted into the Collector.

The configuration used a pattern similar to:

```yaml
receivers:
  filelog:
    include:
      - /var/log/pods/*/*/*.log

    start_at: end

    include_file_path: true
```

### `include`

```yaml
include:
  - /var/log/pods/*/*/*.log
```

This tells the Collector which files should be monitored.

The wildcard pattern allows the Collector to discover logs from many pods and containers without creating separate configuration for every application.

This is important in Kubernetes environments containing many microservices.

We do not configure:

```text
service-a.log
service-b.log
service-c.log
...
```

individually.

Instead, the Collector discovers files using the Kubernetes log directory structure.

---

# `start_at: end`

The configuration included:

```yaml
start_at: end
```

This tells the filelog receiver to begin reading newly discovered files from the end rather than replaying the entire existing file from the beginning.

This can prevent a newly deployed Collector from immediately ingesting a large amount of historical log data.

The appropriate setting may differ depending on operational requirements.

---

# `include_file_path`

The configuration included:

```yaml
include_file_path: true
```

This preserves the source file path as an attribute on the log record.

The file path can also provide useful context when determining which Kubernetes pod/container generated a log record.

---

# OpenTelemetry Logs Pipeline

The Collector configuration connects components through the `service.pipelines` section.

The logs pipeline eventually followed this model:

```yaml
service:
  pipelines:
    logs:
      receivers:
        - otlp
        - filelog

      processors:
        - memory_limiter
        - batch

      exporters:
        - debug
        - otlphttp/loki
```

Conceptually:

```text
filelog
   |
   |
OTLP receiver
   |
   +----------+
              |
              v
       memory_limiter
              |
              v
            batch
              |
        +-----+------+
        |            |
        v            v
      debug      otlphttp/loki
                     |
                     v
                    Loki
```

The OTLP receiver also allows applications capable of producing OTLP logs to send them directly to the Collector.

---

# Memory Limiter Processor

The Collector included:

```yaml
processors:
  memory_limiter:
    check_interval: 5s
    limit_percentage: 80
    spike_limit_percentage: 25
```

The `memory_limiter` processor protects the Collector from excessive memory consumption.

Telemetry volume can increase unexpectedly.

Without memory protection:

```text
Large telemetry volume
        |
        v
Collector memory increases
        |
        v
Container memory limit reached
        |
        v
OOMKilled
```

The memory limiter provides protection against this scenario.

It is therefore generally placed early in the processing pipeline.

---

# Batch Processor

The Collector also used:

```yaml
processors:
  batch: {}
```

Instead of exporting every telemetry record individually, the batch processor can group telemetry before export.

Conceptually:

```text
Log 1 ─┐
Log 2 ─┤
Log 3 ─┤
Log 4 ─┤
Log 5 ─┘
        |
        v
      batch
        |
        v
   Export together
```

Batching can reduce exporter/network overhead and improve efficiency.

---

# Kubernetes Metadata Enrichment

A raw container log is much more useful when Kubernetes context is attached.

Useful metadata can include:

```text
namespace
pod name
container name
node name
deployment/workload
labels
```

The OpenTelemetry `k8sattributes` processor can enrich telemetry with Kubernetes metadata.

Conceptually:

```text
Raw log

"Payment request received"

          |
          v

k8sattributes

          |
          v

Enriched log

message    = "Payment request received"
namespace  = "payment-platform"
pod        = "payment-service-xxxxx"
container  = "payment-service"
node       = "ip-10-0-x-x"
```

The processor communicates with the Kubernetes API and maintains Kubernetes metadata internally.

It does **not** require Redis or a separate metadata-cache service.

Conceptually:

```text
Kubernetes API
      |
      | watch pod metadata
      v
OTel Collector
      |
      +--> in-memory metadata information
                    |
Log record --------+
                    |
                    v
             Enriched record
```

The Collector therefore does not need to perform a completely independent Kubernetes API lookup for every individual log line.

Correct pod association and RBAC configuration are important when implementing this processor.

Metadata enrichment was explored as part of the logging design; it should be configured and verified carefully before relying on particular Kubernetes attributes in production.

---

# Loki Exporter

Loki was configured as an OTLP/HTTP destination:

```yaml
exporters:
  otlphttp/loki:
    endpoint: http://loki.observability.svc.cluster.local:3100/otlp
```

The Kubernetes DNS name:

```text
loki.observability.svc.cluster.local
```

can be broken down as:

```text
loki
 |
 | Service

observability
 |
 | Namespace

svc.cluster.local
 |
 | Kubernetes cluster DNS
```

This allows the Collector to communicate with Loki internally without exposing Loki publicly.

---

# Important OpenTelemetry Pipeline Lesson

One of the most important problems discovered during implementation was that **defining an exporter does not automatically activate it**.

At one point the Collector configuration contained:

```yaml
exporters:
  debug: {}

  otlphttp/loki:
    endpoint: http://loki.observability.svc.cluster.local:3100/otlp
```

This looked correct at first.

However, the rendered logs pipeline contained only:

```yaml
logs:
  exporters:
    - debug
```

Therefore the actual flow was:

```text
filelog
   |
   v
processors
   |
   v
debug
```

rather than:

```text
filelog
   |
   v
processors
   |
   v
Loki
```

The Loki exporter existed, but **nothing in the logs pipeline was sending telemetry to it**.

The fix was to attach the exporter explicitly:

```yaml
service:
  pipelines:
    logs:
      receivers:
        - otlp
        - filelog

      processors:
        - memory_limiter
        - batch

      exporters:
        - debug
        - otlphttp/loki
```

This gives an important OpenTelemetry rule:

> Declaring a receiver, processor, or exporter does not automatically make it part of a telemetry flow. Components must be referenced by the appropriate `service.pipelines` configuration.

---

# Why Keep the Debug Exporter?

During implementation, the `debug` exporter was useful for troubleshooting.

```yaml
exporters:
  debug: {}
```

It allowed received telemetry to be inspected through Collector logs while the Loki integration was being tested.

This created two outputs:

```text
Incoming logs
      |
      v
Processors
      |
      +------> debug
      |
      +------> Loki
```

Once the platform is stable, debug output can be reduced or removed depending on operational requirements because high-volume debug logging can itself create unnecessary overhead.

---

# Verifying the Rendered Collector Configuration

Because the Collector was deployed through:

```text
Git
  ↓
Argo CD
  ↓
Helm
  ↓
Kubernetes
```

we did not rely only on the local `values.yaml`.

The rendered ConfigMap was inspected:

```bash
kubectl get configmap \
  otel-collector-opentelemetry-collector-agent \
  -n observability \
  -o yaml
```

To specifically check the Loki exporter:

```bash
kubectl get configmap \
  otel-collector-opentelemetry-collector-agent \
  -n observability \
  -o yaml | grep -A6 "otlphttp/loki"
```

This exposed an important configuration difference.

The exporter was defined:

```yaml
otlphttp/loki:
  endpoint: http://loki.observability.svc.cluster.local:3100/otlp
```

but initially the rendered logs pipeline showed:

```yaml
logs:
  exporters:
    - debug
```

That proved the problem was in the Collector pipeline rather than Loki itself.

---

# Verifying Loki

Loki's HTTP API was used to verify whether log data had reached the backend.

The following command was executed inside the Loki pod:

```bash
kubectl exec \
  -n observability \
  loki-0 \
  -c loki -- \
  wget -qO- \
  'http://localhost:3100/loki/api/v1/labels'
```

Before successful ingestion, Loki returned:

```json
{
  "status": "success"
}
```

After fixing the OpenTelemetry logs pipeline, the response became:

```json
{
  "status": "success",
  "data": [
    "service_name"
  ]
}
```

This was an important verification point.

It demonstrated that Loki now contained log data with indexed label information.

The logs were subsequently visible through **Grafana Explore**.

---

# Why We Did Not Use Promtail

Promtail was initially considered as the Loki log collector.

However, the platform already contained an OpenTelemetry Collector and the goal was to centralise telemetry collection around OpenTelemetry where practical.

The final logging architecture therefore used:

```text
OTel Collector
      |
      v
     Loki
```

rather than:

```text
Promtail
   |
   v
Loki
```

This reduced the number of separate log collection agents and provided a common Collector architecture that can also participate in metrics and tracing pipelines.

---

# Logs vs Metrics

One important distinction learned during the project is that logs and Prometheus metrics enter the observability system differently.

### Logs

```text
Application
   |
   | stdout/stderr
   v
Node filesystem
   |
   v
/var/log/pods
   |
   | read
   v
OTel filelog receiver
   |
   v
Loki
```

The Collector **reads log files**.

### Application Metrics

```text
Application
   |
   v
Micrometer
   |
   v
/actuator/prometheus
   ^
   |
   | scrape
Prometheus
```

Prometheus **pulls metrics from an HTTP endpoint**.

This difference is important when designing and troubleshooting observability pipelines.

---

# Scaling to Many Applications

The filelog architecture does not require a separate receiver configuration for every microservice.

For example, with 100 application services:

```text
Service A ─┐
Service B ─┤
Service C ─┤
...        ├──> Kubernetes node log files
Service Z ─┘             |
                         v
                   /var/log/pods
                         |
                         v
                 OTel Collector
```

The wildcard filelog configuration discovers matching pod log files on each node.

Kubernetes metadata can then be used to distinguish logs by namespace, pod, container, workload, and other attributes.

This is significantly more scalable than maintaining a manually configured log path for every individual service.

---

# Logging Pipeline Summary

The final verified architecture was:

```text
Application
    |
    | stdout / stderr
    v
Container Runtime
    |
    v
EKS Worker Node
    |
    v
/var/log/pods
    |
    | hostPath
    v
OTel Collector DaemonSet
    |
    v
filelog receiver
    |
    v
memory_limiter
    |
    v
batch
    |
    v
otlphttp/loki
    |
    v
Loki
    |
    v
Grafana Explore
```

---

# Key Lessons

The centralised logging implementation produced several important engineering lessons.

**Kubernetes application logs originate from container stdout/stderr and are made available as files on the worker node.**

**A DaemonSet is appropriate for node-local log collection because it places a Collector on each eligible worker node.**

**`hostPath` allows the Collector pod to access `/var/log/pods` from its node.**

**The `filelog` receiver determines how filesystem logs enter OpenTelemetry.**

**Processors operate on telemetry between ingestion and export.**

**The `memory_limiter` protects Collector memory and `batch` improves export efficiency.**

**Kubernetes metadata enrichment can be performed with the `k8sattributes` processor when pod association and RBAC are correctly configured.**

**An exporter defines a destination, but it does nothing until it is referenced by a telemetry pipeline.**

**The Loki exporter sends logs from the Collector to Loki using OTLP/HTTP.**

**Loki stores and queries logs; Grafana provides the user-facing exploration and visualisation layer.**

**With GitOps and Helm, always inspect the rendered Kubernetes configuration when debugging instead of assuming the local `values.yaml` is what the workload is actually using.**